---
import { siteConfig } from "@/config/site"
import { webmcpToolCount } from "@/lib/developers/webmcp-docs"
import HeadingAnchor from "@/components/heading-anchor"
import DevDocsLayout from "@/astro/layouts/DevDocsLayout.astro"

export const prerender = true

const { title, desc } = siteConfig.metatags.developersWebmcp
---

<DevDocsLayout
  title={title}
  description={desc}
  path="/developers/webmcp"
  eyebrow="WebMCP"
  heading="What is WebMCP"
  intro="Browser tools that let an AI assistant work on your own saved plans, in your own browser. Early preview.">
  <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <div class="bg-primary-subtle space-y-2 rounded-2xl border p-5 text-sm">
      <p class="font-semibold">Early developer preview</p>
      <p>
        WebMCP is a new browser standard that is still being drafted, and
        browsers and assistants are only starting to support it. Today it is for
        testers and developers. Most students will not see any change, and
        NTUMods works exactly as before.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="what">
      What is WebMCP?<HeadingAnchor id="what" label="What is WebMCP?" />
    </h2>
    <p>
      WebMCP lets a website tell a browser assistant what it can do. Today an
      assistant that wants to use a site has to look at the page and guess which
      buttons to press, which is slow and often wrong. With WebMCP the site
      offers a list of actions with clear descriptions, such as "search modules"
      or "add a module to the plan". The assistant picks the right action and
      the site carries it out.
    </p>
    <p>
      To see the difference, try Google's <a
        href="https://googlechromelabs.github.io/webmcp-tools/demos/explainer/#compare"
        >WebMCP comparison demo</a
      >. An assistant books the same appointment twice, once by guessing at the
      page and once by calling the actions the page offers.
    </p>
    <p>
      The standard is being drafted by a W3C community group, with Google and
      Microsoft involved, and Chrome has an early preview. NTUMods offers {
        webmcpToolCount
      } of these actions. They are the same things you can already do on the site,
      so an assistant can never do more than you could do yourself.
    </p>

    <h2 id="versus">
      WebMCP and the MCP server<HeadingAnchor
        id="versus"
        label="WebMCP and the MCP server"
      />
    </h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="data-table text-sm!">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th></th>
            <th>MCP server</th>
            <th>WebMCP</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Runs</td>
            <td>On the NTUMods server</td>
            <td>In your browser tab on ntumods.com</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Works in</td>
            <td>Claude, ChatGPT, Claude Code, Cursor and more</td>
            <td>Browser assistants that support WebMCP</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Sees</td>
            <td>Public module, index and exam data</td>
            <td>The same data, plus your own saved plans</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Can change</td>
            <td>Nothing, it is read-only</td>
            <td>Your plans in your browser, like using the site</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Status</td>
            <td>Ready to use</td>
            <td>Early preview</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      For asking about modules and exams from your own assistant, use the <a
        href="/developers/mcp">MCP server</a
      >. WebMCP is for working on your timetable.
    </p>

    <h2 id="control">
      What stays in your control<HeadingAnchor
        id="control"
        label="What stays in your control"
      />
    </h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Your plans stay in your browser.</strong> The assistant works on the
        plans saved in your own browser, the same ones you see on the site. NTUMods
        does not receive them, and nothing is uploaded to make this work.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hard-to-undo actions ask first.</strong> Clearing a plan, or replacing
        a plan with a shared timetable, shows a confirmation that you answer, not
        the assistant.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Everything else is easy to reverse.</strong> Adding, removing and
        swapping modules works the same as doing it by hand, and you can switch between
        your five plans as usual.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It is optional.</strong> If your browser or assistant does not support
        it, nothing changes and nothing extra is loaded.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="limits">
      What it does not do<HeadingAnchor
        id="limits"
        label="What it does not do"
      />
    </h2>
    <p>
      NTUMods does not register you for modules. STARS does that. The data also
      has no live index vacancies and no exam venues, so an assistant cannot
      know either. Treat any plan an assistant builds as a draft, and check it
      against STARS before registration opens. Assistants can misread a request,
      so look at the plan on screen before you rely on it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="why">
      Why NTUMods offers it<HeadingAnchor
        id="why"
        label="Why NTUMods offers it"
      />
    </h2>
    <p>
      More students now ask an AI assistant for help with planning, and
      assistants are getting better at doing tasks, not only answering
      questions. When an assistant guesses at a web page, it can pick the wrong
      index, miss a clash or quote last semester's data. When it uses actions
      the site provides, it uses the same data and the same clash checks you see
      on screen.
    </p>
    <p>
      Next: see <a href="/developers/webmcp/examples">what you can ask</a>, <a
        href="/developers/webmcp/try">try it yourself</a
      >, or read the <a href="/developers/webmcp/tools">list of tools</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</DevDocsLayout>
